The load balancer receives HTTP requests and forwards them to healthy registered targets. The Auto Scaling Group maintains the configured capacity and uses the CPU target-tracking policy to adjust capacity according to demand.

## 5. Launch Template Configuration

The launch template `datacenter-launch-template` defines the EC2 instance configuration.

- **AMI:** Amazon Linux 2023
- **Instance type:** `t2.micro`
- **Web server:** Nginx
- **Inbound HTTP:** TCP port 80, permitted from the load balancer security group
- **User data:** Installs, enables, and starts Nginx

User-data script:

```bash
#!/bin/bash
dnf install -y nginx
systemctl enable --now nginx
```

This script installs Nginx and configures it to start automatically at boot.

## 6. Auto Scaling Group Configuration

The Auto Scaling Group is named `datacenter-asg`.

| Setting | Value |
|---|---:|
| Minimum size | 1 |
| Desired capacity | 1 |
| Maximum size | 2 |
| CPU target | 50% |
| Health check type | ELB |

The group is associated with the `datacenter-tg` target group. The target-tracking policy is named `datacenter-cpu-target-tracking`.

The policy is configured to maintain average CPU utilization around 50%, adjusting the number of instances within the configured minimum and maximum limits as needed.

## 7. Target Group and Load Balancer

The target group `datacenter-tg` uses HTTP on port 80 and performs health checks to determine whether the registered EC2 instance can receive traffic.

The internet-facing Application Load Balancer `datacenter-alb` has an HTTP listener on port 80 that forwards requests to the target group.

**Load balancer URL:**

http://datacenter-alb-1873907469.us-east-1.elb.amazonaws.com

